<?php require __DIR__.'/../includes/bootstrap.php';$post=post_by_slug('claude-opus-5-5-what-changed') ?? ['slug'=>'claude-opus-5-5-what-changed','title'=>'Claude Opus 5.5: What Changed and How Teams Can Use It Now','description'=>'Anthropic launched Claude Opus 5.5 on September 22. Learn its pricing, model fit, benchmark limits and how teams should test it in real workflows now.','category'=>'Claude','date'=>'2026-09-25','read_time'=>'9 min read'];ob_start(); ?><p>Anthropic introduced Claude Opus 5.5 on September 22, 2026. The company describes it as the first model in its Claude 5.5 family and says it leads on agentic coding, computer use and knowledge work in its benchmark set. Anthropic also says Opus 5.5 costs 40% less to run than Opus 5 on typical workloads.</p><figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/claude-opus-5-5-what-changed.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Claude Opus 5.5 positioned for coding, agentic work and knowledge tasks"></figure><h2>What changed</h2><p>Opus 5.5 targets work that requires several steps instead of one short answer. Anthropic highlights coding, computer use and knowledge work. It is a candidate for large codebases, research, document workflows and agents that need careful tool use.</p><p>It should not automatically become the default model for every request. Short extraction and low-risk classification may be better served by a cheaper model. A tiered model strategy can be more efficient than using one model everywhere.</p><h2>Pricing and total cost</h2><p>Anthropic lists Opus 5.5 at $4 per million input tokens and $20 per million output tokens. Cache pricing can reduce the cost of repeated context. Real spend also depends on prompt size, output, tools and retries.</p><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Token price</td><td>Starting model cost</td></tr><tr><td>Cache</td><td>Repeated context may cost less</td></tr><tr><td>Retries</td><td>Agent failures multiply spend</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Correction time can dominate cost</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How to read the benchmarks</h2><p>Anthropic says Opus 5.5 leads on several evaluations while also noting that benchmark margins are becoming a less reliable guide to real-world differences at this capability level. A benchmark uses defined tasks and settings; your production system has different data, tools, permissions and failure modes.</p><p>Build a representative evaluation set before migrating. Include incomplete inputs, long context, tool failures and ambiguous instructions. Record whether the final result is usable without major editing.</p><h2>A practical evaluation</h2><ol><li>Collect real tasks from coding, research, support or content.</li><li>Remove private information while keeping task structure.</li><li>Define acceptance rules before testing.</li><li>Run multiple attempts.</li><li>Record tool errors and human correction.</li><li>Calculate cost per finished task.</li></ol><h2>Agentic coding needs controls</h2><p>When Opus 5.5 runs inside a coding agent, it may inspect files, edit code and use development tools. Keep read, write and deployment permissions separate. Run tests before merge and review the complete diff. Never expose production credentials simply because an agent can use them.</p><h2>What SEO teams can test</h2><p>SEO teams can evaluate Opus 5.5 on large-site audits, query clustering, internal-link planning, technical documentation and research synthesis. Score source accuracy, link accuracy, requirement coverage, false positives and editor time. The key metric is verified work completed with less correction.</p><p>See <a href="/claude/claude-financial-advisors-workflow">Claude for Financial Advisors: What the New Workflow Means</a>.</p><h2>Safeguards and limits</h2><p>Anthropic says Opus 5.5 uses safeguards developed for its most capable systems. Application controls still matter because your software decides what data the model can access and which tools it can call. Availability and limits can differ by product and cloud deployment.</p><h2>Practical takeaways</h2><ul><li>Opus 5.5 launched September 22, 2026.</li><li>Anthropic says it costs 40% less than Opus 5 on typical workloads.</li><li>Benchmark wins do not guarantee your production result.</li><li>Measure completed-task cost and correction time.</li><li>Keep agent permissions narrower than developer permissions.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is Claude Opus 5.5?</h3><p>It is Anthropic's new Opus-tier model in the Claude 5.5 family, aimed at agentic coding, computer use and knowledge work.</p><h3>How much does it cost?</h3><p>Anthropic lists $4 per million input tokens and $20 per million output tokens.</p><h3>Should a business switch all workloads?</h3><p>No. Test representative tasks and compare quality, latency, correction time and total cost.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5">Anthropic — Introducing Claude Opus 5.5</a></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news">Anthropic — News and research</a></li></ul><?php $articleHtml=ob_get_clean();require __DIR__.'/../includes/blog-template.php';